AVM: usbredirhost sends an interrupt OUT transfer longer than one packet
to the device whole, instead of refusing it

usbredirhost refused every interrupt OUT transfer longer than the
endpoint's wMaxPacketSize ("error received interrupt out packet is
larger than wMaxPacketSize") and answered the guest usb_redir_inval.
USB allows such a transfer: the host controller sends it as a run of
packets, and QEMU's xHCI hands usb-redir each guest transfer whole. A
HID device's output report goes as one interrupt OUT transfer, so a
report longer than the endpoint's packet size never reached the device.
An APH Mantis Q40 sent to a Windows machine through AVM's USB helper
typed into Windows, while NVDA and JAWS, which write to it, never found
it (2026-10-29). The same error from a Windows 10 guest, through SPICE's
client, is on the spice-devel list (2020-10, "wMaxPacketSize"),
unanswered.

libusb's macOS backend submits an interrupt transfer of any length
(WritePipeAsync), and the parser holds data_len to the header's 16-bit
length. Applies to usbredir 0.14.0, the release UTM's build uses. AVM's
USB helper is the only user of usbredirhost in the sysroot; QEMU uses
only the parser.

--- a/usbredirhost/usbredirhost.c
+++ b/usbredirhost/usbredirhost.c
@@ -2513,14 +2513,13 @@
         return;
     }
 
-    if (data_len > host->endpoint[EP2I(ep)].max_packetsize) {
-        ERROR("error received interrupt out packet is larger than wMaxPacketSize");
-        usbredirhost_send_interrupt_status(host, id, interrupt_packet,
-                                           usb_redir_inval);
-        usbredirparser_free_packet_data(host->parser, data);
-        FLUSH(host);
-        return;
-    }
+    /* AVM: a transfer longer than one packet goes to the device whole, and
+       the host controller splits it into packets, as USB does for any
+       interrupt OUT transfer. Refused, every HID output report longer than
+       the endpoint's wMaxPacketSize failed: Windows sends each report as one
+       transfer, so NVDA and JAWS never reached a USB braille display
+       (2026-10-29, an APH Mantis Q40). The parser holds data_len to the
+       header's 16-bit length. */
 
     usbredirhost_log_data(host, "interrupt data out:", data, data_len);
 
